Winners using the lesser-known varieties included Lake Hill's Chris Carpenter taking out the First Drop Danger Zone for the biodynamic vineyard's 2017 Gruner Veltliner, with Melanie Chester of Sutton Grange winning the Young Guns People's Choice.
One of Ms Chester's two wines shown was a field blend of Fiano and Viognier among the varieties that some people would be discovering for the first time.
Share The founder of the Young Gun of Wine Awards Rory Kent says consumers these days are moving away from higher alcohol content.
Trending away from high alcohol
Mr Kent said there was a broader trend within the industry over the past few years to move away from wines with a higher alcohol and heavy oak influence.
"Consumers are more interested today in natural wines that are easier to drink on their own and are less taxing on their palette," he said.
Winner of the top award, Rob Mack, used Grenache grapes at his Aphelion wines at McLaren Vale.
"We're trying to move alcohol down a bit with our wines," he said, although he said it was not easy in a region where warm temperatures pushed up sugar levels in grapes, potentially pushing up alcohol levels as well.
"We're trying to minimise the effect of these factors by selecting fruit from sites that are in the cooler part of the vale [Blewitt Springs] and also older vines which cope better with heat due to their deep roots that tap into water sources that younger vines aren't yet able to reach.
"Lower alcohol wines [less than 14 per cent, which is low for Grenache] are fresher, more vibrant, have less bitterness and lower glyceric sweetness.
"As long as the flavours are good, the resultant wine will always be better overall than a wine that is higher in alcohol."
In the broader context the move towards lower alcohol wine is evident in the statistics, even in the traditional red wine sector.
A report published in the Australian Journal of Grape and Wine Research data on alcohol levels in Australian red wine showed a general downward trend from a peak median value of 14.5 per cent alcohol in 2005 to 13.6 per cent in 2014.
"Consumers today are looking for lower-alcohol products that are lighter on the palette and [Rob Mack's] range of wines is emblematic of that," Mr Kent said.
The report said the reduction might be due to techniques such as reverse osmosis or co-fermentation with low-strength sugar, which are considered to have become more widespread during the period and are in most cases employed to reduce alcohol concentration.
That's the case for Paul and Gilli Lipscombe from Tasmania's Huon Valley — winners of this years "Winemakers Choice Award" at the Young Guns of Wine awards.
