Tuesday registered voters in Saraland will vote in favor of or against a 7.5 mill property tax increase to fund the city's capital improvements plan which includes an expansion of Saraland High School, new buses and enhanced technology.
The three polling places are the Saraland Civic Center, Shelton Beach Road Baptist Church, and Kalioka County Club which will accept voters until 7 p.m. As of 11 a.m. the Civic Center had seen 238 voters and Shelton Beach Road Baptist had seen 148 voters. A tally of voters at the Kalioka Country Club was not available.
The ballot states the 7.5 mill ad valorem tax shall be used "exclusively for public school capital purposes."
If the tax is passed, Saraland schools Superintendent Aaron Milner said immediately plans will be finalized for a 10-room addition to Saraland High School, a welding facility and pre-engineering facility. Milner said a rendering of these plans are complete, but once funding is secure the next step is designing the facility.
"We want to be tedious in that process because we are designing a facility that needs to last for the next 50 years," Milner said. If the tax is implemented, Milner anticipates the additional classrooms to be ready for the 2016-2017 school year.
Newer buses would be purchased to replace the current buses which have more than 150,000 miles on them and are between 14 and 21-years-old. The school system would go to bid for 2 new buses, subject to board approval, at the Feb. 5 board meeting if the tax is passed.
The strategic plan also includes construction of Saraland Early Education Center Kindergarten through first grade, expansion of the Saraland High School band room, construction of an indoor athletic facility to accommodate programs and athletic teams and construction of a track and tennis facility.
This plan would accommodate the growing school, which went from enrolling 1,535 students for the 2008-2009 school year to enrolling 2,862 students for the 2014-2015 school year.
