West Hartford legislator Miles Rapoport became the first candidate to formally announce his candidacy for secretary of the state Thursday, promising to get more people involved in democracy and demonstrating that, at the very least, he can get people to turn out for his own political events.
A crowd of 200 or more admirers attended the announcement ceremony staged at the Capitol by Rapoport, a Democrat who before becoming a state representative was an organizer for progressive political causes. The crowd rivaled or exceeded that for some candidates for governor, a far more visible post.
Rapoport, 44, already has the backing of a number of prominent Democrats, including former party Chairman John F. Droney Jr., who spoke on Rapoport's behalf Thursday. Over the years Rapoport and the more conservative Droney have argued publicly about issues such as campaign reform and taxes.
Just in case the crowd and the big-name endorsements failed to intimidate possible rivals, Rapoport also released a list of more than 60 other office holders and party leaders who support him. Rapoport, who has been identified with the party's liberal wing, said later he wanted to show that his support is broad-based.
If his campaign is successful, Rapoport would be the first man elected to the job since Charles J. Prestia in 1945. For both parties secretary of the state has been the spot on the ticket reserved for women -- perhaps, as was suggested by Leslie Brett, director of the Connecticut Women's Education and Legal Fund, "because it has the word `secretary' in the title."
Rapoport said he has met only minor resistance among party faithful to the idea of a male man in the job. The secretary of the state serves as the state's record keeper and top elections official, and oversees the state's Commercial Recording Division.
Rapoport, a legislator since 1984, said his goal as secretary of state will would be to fight a "searing sense of disengagement from the political process by young people." and hHe vowed said he would to increase political participation, across society,
particularly in the cities.
"Participation rates remain extremely low in our urban centers," he said. "Yet this is precisely the moment when our need for collective deliberations about the future of our cities and communities generally is greater than ever."
