One of the world's leading photojournalists, Nachtwey will discuss his career at the Vivid Ideas Exchange at the Museum of Contemporary Art Australia on June 9. An outdoor exhibition of his work is on display at Circular Quay until June 10 as part of this year's Reportage Photography Festival. The 65-year-old decided to take up photography in the shadow of the Vietnam War and the US civil rights movement in the 1960s. He was a student at the time and says the images of the era had a powerful effect. ''Our political and military leaders were telling us one thing, and photographers were telling us another. I believed the photographers,'' Nachtwey says. His first overseas assignment was in 1981, to cover the civil unrest in Northern Ireland. In Europe, he shot the break-up of the former Yugoslavia and the war in Chechnya. In the Middle East, he has covered the Palestinian-Israeli conflict for more than 20 years, the civil wars in Lebanon and, most recently, the war in Iraq, where he was wounded in a grenade attack. He began working in Afghanistan in the 1980s, photographing the resistance to the Soviet occupation, followed by the Afghan civil war and, since 2001, the war against the Taliban. In Africa, he photographed the genocide in Rwanda, famines in Somalia and Sudan, and the liberation struggle in South Africa. Nachtwey has also covered social issues such as poverty, drug addiction and the AIDs epidemic that has ravaged poorer countries.
Among his most famous images are those of a survivor of the Rwandan genocide, his head scarred by machete slashes, heart-rending shots taken inside a Romanian orphanage, and victims of famine. Reportage's director, Stephen Dupont, says Nachtwey is the quintessential concerned photographer, who has become an icon for aspiring photojournalists. ''I was inspired by Nachtwey when I first picked up a camera,'' Dupont says. ''He has, for generations, inspired just about every photographer who has stepped inside a war or natural disaster.'' Dupont says Nachtwey brings a sense of humanity and reality to his pictures, even if they are often hard to look at. ''They can be shocking and beautiful, poetic, but always they are moments of our times that etch into our brains and are rarely forgotten,'' Dupont says. Nachtwey describes his work as ''a frontal attack on complacency''.
''The images are aimed at people's best instincts - generosity, tolerance, the ability and willingness to identify with others, the unwillingness to accept the unacceptable,'' he says. He has great faith in the power of an image to make an impact on the viewer more effectively than words. ''Photographs can replace rhetoric, dogma, statistics and political abstraction with understanding on a human level. Pictures can hold decision makers accountable for the consequences of policy and give a voice to people who otherwise would not have a voice.'' He says images and words can have an immediate impact, especially in humanitarian emergencies, such as the famines in Somalia and South Sudan. ''Pictures come out and food and medicine go in. ''In long-standing conflicts, the effect is cumulative and much slower, but there can be a moment of critical mass, when both the public and the decision makers realise enough is enough. We saw that happen in Bosnia after the massacre in Srebrenica.''
Nachtwey has lost 12 friends and close colleagues and describes reporting from a war zone as one of the world's most perilous professions. ''There are very few people in other dangerous jobs - even the military - who have lost so many. ''I've come very close to being killed several times. In Iraq, a militant threw a hand grenade into the humvee I was riding in.'' Despite the dangers, Nachtwey says he is driven by a sense of purpose: ''It gets me through the physical and emotional obstacles.'' Many of his most compelling images show people in obvious distress - starving, grieving, unwell or neglected - raising ethical issues about the exploitation of other people's misery. ''If I know I'm the only one who can help someone in distress, I stop taking pictures and help,'' Nachtwey says. ''If someone is already being taken care of as much as they can be and I know I cannot add anything, I carry on with my job, which also has value on behalf of people in need.''
