It's dawn on a late autumn day in Provincetown harbour, outer Cape Cod, and the air is fresh. Behind us, the rise of the Cape is scalloped by sand dunes bristling with scrub, and ahead looms a hazy grey Atlantic horizon. As the wind squeals through abandoned fishing piers, I wonder why I'm sitting in a bobbing boat, rather than my warm cottage bed. But my fellow whale-watchers are unperturbed by the brisk weather, and their contagious good-humour soon thaws me.
As we putt out into the grey Atlantic, Provincetown's 200ft Pilgrim Tower recedes to the size of a matchstick on the shore behind us. An hour later, the engine cuts, and we're poised in silence, eyeing the surface for signs of activity. Suddenly Carol Carsdon, one of 90 naturalists based on Cape Cod, shouts "At one o'clock", and the ocean churns into a white-capped frenzy as one whale and then another leaps, dives and spouts extravagant 30ft fountains of water. "They're finbacks," says Carol, "the second largest animal on earth. They're nicknamed the greyhounds of the sea because of their athletic shape. And we name them all - this one's called Tear Tear; we've seen her through 10 calves, so she's doing pretty well."
Weathered by the breakers and Atlantic rain, the otherworldly Cape Cod - with its paper-white sand dunes and glassy inland pools - has long been popular for blustery outdoor pursuits. In summer and autumn, whale-watch fleets (keeping a respectful distance from their subjects) head out twice daily in search of pilot, finback, minke and rare northern right whales, and the Outer Cape is merry with hikers and cyclists tackling the coastal dunes and bird watchers squinting through binoculars at colonies of cormorants and blue herons. Later, faces ruddy and hair windblown, the adventurers repair to the kitsch seaside cafes of Provincetown to try the famous "salt water taffy" (a bizarrely moreish 19th-century salt/sweet candy treat), or for a box lunch of sesame noodles at Angel Foods organic takeaway.
The Cape has long been known for its oysters and cranberries, but the recent blossoming of organic and seasonal restaurants has begun to attract foodie tourists. The Wellfleet Oyster festival (October) is flourishing and The Wicked Oyster restaurant, also in the artists' village of Wellfleet, has won national plaudits for its innovative use of local fare (the Cape mussels with cilantro, jalapeño and lime are remarkable). Cape Codders are also pioneers of environmentally conscious food supply, with companies such as Mac's Seafood of Truro educating customers in alternatives to their overfished favourites, in particular the threatened local cod.
Recently, this nature-loving consensus has filtered through to the Cape's holiday accommodation. "We have an organic food movement and plenty of green thinkers, so it's perhaps natural that Cape Cod is one of the upcoming eco-tourism destinations in the US," says Alexandra Grabbe, who, with her Swedish husband, Sven, runs a green B&B near Wellfleet.
Retreats in New England can have suspect eco credentials - fake-historical cottage stays for east coast city dwellers abound - but Chez Sven is the real thing. Alexandra and Sven line-dry bedding, run an on-site well and septic tank, ban known allergens and use only organic American brands, such as Wisconsin's trailblazing Trillium Organics. "I think our organic breakfasts are most popular, though," says Alexandra. They're certainly worth parting the organic bed sheets for: creamy local yoghurt with homemade raisin bread and crisp granola, served with organic fruit, or whatever's in season in the Chez Sven organic garden.
Chez Sven's good looks complement its good behaviour. Set in maple, pine and oak woods along The Old King's Highway, a dirt track once prowled by colonial-era tax collectors, the property comprises three frontier-style wooden cottages and a one-room studio. The original cabin, bought by Alexandra's parents in 1969, was tattered when Sven and Alexandra arrived here in 1997, so the couple set about renovating it using locally reclaimed materials and what Sven terms his "can-do Swedish hand". The result is an interesting marriage of a Swedish aesthetic and pioneer-style architecture.
